\section{The grid}{

Rows are read in logical order -- every \verb{<thead>}, then the \verb{<tbody>}
sections (and any rows directly in the table), then every \verb{<tfoot>} --
and footer rows are ordinary data rows. Rows of nested tables never
become rows of the outer table, and a nested table's text is left out of
the cell that holds it (it still separates the text around it with a
line break).

Each cell is placed at the next free column of its row. \code{colspan} and
\code{rowspan} are read with HTML's rules for non-negative integers: a
missing or invalid value is 1, \code{colspan="0"} is 1, and values are capped
at 1000 columns and 65534 rows. \code{rowspan="0"} extends to the end of the
cell's row group, and a larger \code{rowspan} is cut silently at the end of
its group. A spanned value is repeated in every slot it covers. A cell
whose span would cover a slot another cell already covers is an error of
class \code{zuhtml_table_structure_error}, never a silent overwrite. Slots no
cell covers, in ragged rows, are \code{NA}; an empty cell is \code{""}.
}

\section{Headers}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"auto"}: the rows of \verb{<thead>} sections if there are any, otherwise
the leading rows whose cells are all \verb{<th>} (a \verb{<th>} among \verb{<td>}s
does not make a header row);
\item \code{TRUE}: the first row; \code{FALSE}: none;
\item a vector of row numbers: those rows.
